Answer: Shadowing occurs when a local variable or constructor parameter has the same name as an instance variable. For example:

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
```

Demo(int a, int b) {

```java
a = a;
b = b;
}
}
```

Here, the constructor parameters a and b have the same names as the instance variables. The statements:

```java
a = a;
b = b;
```

refer to the local parameters on both sides. Therefore, the values are not assigned to the instance variables. This is called the shadowing problem. The this keyword is used to resolve this problem.

Answer: The this keyword refers to the current object. Therefore, this.a and this.b refer to the instance variables of the current object. The corrected constructor is:

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
```

Demo(int a, int b) {

```java
this.a = a;
this.b = b;
}
}
```

Here: this.a → instance variable a → constructor parameter this.b → instance variable b → constructor parameter Therefore, the values received by the constructor are correctly assigned to the instance variables.

Answer: Constructor chaining is the process of calling one constructor from another constructor. Within the same class, constructor chaining is performed using this(). For example:

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
```

Demo() {

```java
this(10, 20);
}
```

Demo(int a, int b) {

```java
this.a = a;
this.b = b;
}
}
```

Here, the no-argument constructor calls the parameterized constructor using:

```java
this(10, 20);
```

The control is transferred to the constructor that accepts two parameters.

Answer: this() must call another constructor of the same class, but the constructor chain must eventually terminate. For example, this is invalid:

```java
class Demo {
```

Demo() {

```java
this(10);
}
```

Demo(int a) {

```java
this();
}
}
```

The constructors continuously call each other: Demo() ↓ Demo(int) ↓ Demo() ↓ Demo(int) ↓ ... This creates recursive constructor invocation. Java reports this as a recursive constructor invocation error. Therefore, at least one constructor in the chain must eventually stop the this() chain.

Answer: Constructor overloading allows different objects of the same class to be initialized with different sets of values. For example:

```java
class Demo {
int a;
int b;
```

Demo() {

```java
this.a = 10;
this.b = 20;
}
```

Demo(int a, int b) {

```java
this.a = a;
this.b = b;
}
}
```

Different objects can now be initialized in different ways. Constructor overloading is useful when different objects require different initial states.

Answer: A copy constructor is a constructor that accepts an object of the same class type as its parameter. It is used to create a new object by copying values from an existing object. For example:

```java
class Student {
int id;
```

Student(int id) {

```java
this.id = id;
}
```

Student(Student s) {

```java
this.id = s.id;
}
}
```

The copy constructor is: Student(Student s) The parameter s is of the same type as the class, Student. An object can be copied as follows:

```java
Student s1 = new Student(101);
Student s2 = new Student(s1);
```

Here, the values of s1 are copied into s2.

Answer: Consider a class containing all these elements:

```java
class Sample {
static int a = 10;
static int b = 20;
static {
System.out.println("Static block executed");
}
static void display1() {
System.out.println("Static method executed");
System.out.println(a + " " + b);
}
int x;
int y;
{
x = 100;
y = 200;
System.out.println("Instance block executed");
}
```

Sample() {

```java
System.out.println("Constructor executed");
}
```

void display2() {

```java
System.out.println("Instance method executed");
}
}
```

If the application contains:

```java
class SampleApp {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Sample.display1();
Sample sample = new Sample();
sample.display2();
}
}
```

The execution order is: Static block executed Static method executed 10 20 Instance block executed Constructor executed Instance method executed The static block executes during class loading. The static method is then called without creating an object. When the object is created, the instance block executes before the constructor. Finally, the instance method executes when it is called.

Answer: Both can be used for initializing instance variables, but they are used for different purposes. Consider a Student class having: name id gender maritalStatus Suppose name, id, and gender are different for every student, while maritalStatus should initially be false for every student. The constructor can initialize values that are specific to each object:

```java
class Student {
String name;
int id;
String gender;
boolean maritalStatus;
```

Student(String name, int id, String gender) {

```java
this.name = name;
this.id = id;
this.gender = gender;
}
}
```

The instance block can initialize a value that should receive the same initial value whenever an object is created:

```java
{
maritalStatus = false;
}
```

Thus: Object-specific values → Constructor Common initial instance values → Instance block The constructor receives object-specific values through parameters, whereas the instance block executes automatically whenever an object is created.

Answer: A variable should be declared as static when its value is common to all objects of the class. For example, the value of PI is common when calculating the area of different shapes.

```java
class Shape {
static double PI = 3.147;
}
```

The same value can be used by objects representing different shapes. Another example is nationality when every object represents a person with the same nationality:

```java
static String nationality = "Indian";
```

The important rule is: Value common to all objects → static variable Value specific to an object → instance variable

Answer: A static variable can be used to maintain the count because the count must be common to all objects. The constructor can increment the count whenever an object is created.

```java
class Demo {
static int count = 0;
```

Demo() {

```java
count++;
}
}
class DemoApp {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Demo d1 = new Demo();
Demo d2 = new Demo();
Demo d3 = new Demo();
System.out.println(Demo.count);
}
}
```

Output: 3 The variable count is static, so only one common copy is maintained for the class. Every time the constructor executes, count is incremented. The static variable should be accessed using the class name: Demo.count rather than through an object reference.

Answer: Suppose the class contains a name, term, and constructor:

```java
class ChiefMinister {
String name;
int term;
```

ChiefMinister(String name, int term) {

```java
this.name = name;
this.term = term;
}
}
```

To convert it into a Singleton class, the constructor is made private and a private static reference is created.

```java
class ChiefMinister {
String name;
int term;
private ChiefMinister(String name, int term) {
this.name = name;
this.term = term;
}
private static ChiefMinister ref;
public static ChiefMinister getInstance(String name, int term) {
if (ref == null) {
ref = new ChiefMinister(name, term);
}
return ref;
}
}
```

The object is obtained through the static method:

```java
ChiefMinister cm = ChiefMinister.getInstance("ABC", 10);
```

The constructor cannot be called directly from outside the class:

```java
ChiefMinister cm = new ChiefMinister("ABC", 10);
```

because the constructor is private. If the object already exists, getInstance() returns the existing reference instead of creating another object.
